# Test: ze doctor reports a missing hugepage reservation when VPP is enabled
# Spec: plan/spec-vpp-host-tuning.md AC-9
# VALIDATES: with vpp.enabled and no hugepages reserved (the fixture root has no
#            hugepages sysfs tree), `ze doctor --json` surfaces
#            doctor-vpp-hugepages end-to-end; a disabled vpp config is silent.
# Linux-only: the hugepage check lives in doctor_linux.go. Its procfs/sysfs roots
# are redirected via ze.test.doctor.hugepages-root so the test is hermetic and
# does not depend on the host's real hugepage configuration.

option=skip-os:value=aix,android,darwin,dragonfly,freebsd,illumos,ios,js,netbsd,openbsd,plan9,solaris,wasip1,windows
option=env:var=ze.test.doctor.hugepages-root:value=nohugepages

tmpfs=vpp-on.conf:terminator=EOF_ON
vpp {
	enabled true;
}
EOF_ON

tmpfs=vpp-off.conf:terminator=EOF_OFF
vpp {
	enabled false;
}
EOF_OFF

# // test-relax: the per-command assertion changes 0 -> 1. No coverage is lost;
# the expectation was self-contradictory. "VPP enabled and no hugepages
# reserved" is emitted at severity ERROR (evaluateVPPHugepages,
# internal/component/vpp/doctor_linux.go), an error diagnostic makes the report
# not-ready, and `ze doctor` exits 1 when it is not ready. So the old exit=0
# asserted that the check does NOT fire, contradicting the stdout assertion on
# the next line: the test could not pass whatever the code did. It went
# unnoticed because the file is skip-os everywhere but Linux and had therefore
# never run until CI existed. Asserting exit=1 pins the real contract -- a
# missing reservation is a readiness FAILURE, not a note.
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze doctor --json vpp-on.conf:exit=1
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:contains=doctor-vpp-hugepages

cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze doctor --json vpp-off.conf:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:not-contains=doctor-vpp-hugepages
